```json
[
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 92,
    "summary": "The new P3 exit criterion requires bug-pair discrimination \"on every usable pair\", but RFC-0007 says Tier 0 currently has no usable bug pair.",
    "failure_scenario": "RFC-0007 (Corpus section) says \"Tier 0 has no usable bug pair today, and E0 cannot measure M2. M2 starts at E1\". GEPA v1 (P3) runs on Tier 0 plus mutants (per the RFC E4 note). If E1 has not landed, \"every usable pair\" is an empty set and the clause passes vacuously. P3 could then be declared done with no M2 measurement, which is the guard the clause was added for. The criterion needs a minimum count, or it needs to name E1 as a P3 dependency."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 90,
    "summary": "The P3 exit demands \"bootstrap 95% CIs\" unconditionally, but RFC-0007 says to compute bootstrap intervals \"only once N supports them\".",
    "failure_scenario": "The held-out Tier 0 set is about 6 kernels with 3 samples per cell. A bootstrap CI at that N is meaningless or degenerate. A mandatory CI either forces a spurious interval to satisfy the exit, or P3 can never formally exit. The roadmap and the RFC give opposite rules for the same statistic."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 90,
    "summary": "The P3 exit hard-codes the blind and sighted recipes from RFC-0007, which is a Draft/RFC (\"not yet an ADR\"), and no P2 or P3 item schedules the work that builds them.",
    "failure_scenario": "The blind and sighted recipes, UNKNOWN/vacuous/skipped counts and M2 come from RFC-0007's E0 and E1. E0 needs a minimal T1/T2/T4 harness. The roadmap's P2 and P3 bullets do not mention E0 or E1. If the RFC changes or is rejected, the exit criterion points at a moving target. If E0 and E1 are not scheduled, P3 cannot be satisfied by its own deadline of 2026-12-18."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 58,
    "summary": "The banner says P4 absorbs the slip, but only P4's dates moved. P4's scope is unchanged and has grown, and P5 is still labelled \"Dec–Jan\" and overlaps it.",
    "failure_scenario": "ADR-0006 says P4's real-code scope absorbs the slip. This change instead moves P4's window to about 2026-12-19 to 2027-01-15 (about 4 weeks), keeps its exit criterion, and the RFC note adds E2–E4 (Tier 1 replay, kernel pilot, second GEPA pass) to P4. The P4 and P5 headings both read \"Dec–Jan\" and P5's bar date is 2027-01-31, so the two phases overlap. Nothing says what is cut if P4 overruns again. The P5 text is also stale: \"Lean spike only if Jesse committed in Sep\" and risk 8's \"Sep scoping chat\" refer to a month already past."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/roadmap.md",
    "line": 60,
    "summary": "The roadmap now hard-codes GitHub milestone due dates, although it says live tracking lives on GitHub (ADR-0005).",
    "failure_scenario": "The intro of docs/roadmap.md says live task tracking is on GitHub, with milestones as phases. The same dates are now copied into prose, along with a \"Re-dated 2026-10-26\" banner. The next milestone edit on GitHub will silently contradict the roadmap, and the roadmap will drift. The banner's causal claim (\"P2 and P3 overran: W3 and W4 had no sub-issues yet\") also reads as the cause of the overrun when it is only a symptom."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/design/0007-what-should-we-prove-proposer-at-project-scale.md",
    "line": 239,
    "summary": "The new E4 note attributes \"GEPA v1 runs first on Tier 0 plus mutants\" to ADR-0006, which does not say that. It also conflicts with the RFC's own held-out split of edit events reserved for GEPA.",
    "failure_scenario": "ADR-0006 only says property-gen, grading and GEPA (P2–P3) go first \"on controllable kernels\". It does not say \"Tier 0 plus mutants\", and it does not describe E4 as a second GEPA pass. The Corpus section of the RFC says \"A held-out split of edit events is reserved for GEPA (#5) from the start\", but Tier 0 has no bug pairs or edit events. A reader cannot tell what GEPA v1 optimises against or what E4 re-optimises. The note also calls E2–E4 \"P4's real-code work\", yet E4 is a GEPA pass, not real-code work. Because accepted ADRs cannot be edited, a misquoted citation here cannot be fixed at the source."
  }
]
```
